    "knowledge_point": "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent",
    "problem": "$\\int \\sqrt{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=5x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(5x+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x+1)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(5x+1)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5x+1}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
